1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:06,643
We stopped one container, the frontend,
and restarted the web server in front of it.

2
00:00:06,963 --> 00:00:11,293
The API container is still up,
and so is the vector database.

3
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:15,800
Yet the whole site answers nothing,
the address of the API included.

4
00:00:16,033 --> 00:00:20,534
Today's puzzle: the API is running,
so why is its address dead too?

5
00:00:20,854 --> 00:00:23,543
We answer it once
we have built this by hand.

6
00:00:23,783 --> 00:00:27,371
Here is the goal,
the lecture's picture of deployment:

7
00:00:27,371 --> 00:00:32,421
the containers from your laptop move
to one virtual machine in Google Cloud,

8
00:00:32,421 --> 00:00:34,215
with port 80 the only door.

9
00:00:34,535 --> 00:00:37,138
We build it twice: by hand, then as code.

10
00:00:37,383 --> 00:00:42,354
On the course roadmap, the marker has moved
to deployment, scaling and automation.

11
00:00:42,674 --> 00:00:45,362
Everything so far ran on your own computer.

12
00:00:45,682 --> 00:00:47,133
Users need a server.

13
00:00:47,367 --> 00:00:51,162
You need cheese-app-v3
with your secrets folder beside it,

14
00:00:51,162 --> 00:00:55,218
a Google Cloud project with billing,
and a Docker Hub account.

15
00:00:55,538 --> 00:00:59,825
This costs real money,
so each part ends by deleting everything.

16
00:01:00,067 --> 00:01:03,184
The tracker in the corner
names the README step,

17
00:01:03,184 --> 00:01:05,651
code panels name their file and lines,

18
00:01:05,651 --> 00:01:09,027
and a yellow tag,
your turn, means pause and run it.

19
00:01:09,267 --> 00:01:10,352
One honest note:

20
00:01:10,352 --> 00:01:14,963
our recording machine has no Google account
and no Docker Hub login.

21
00:01:15,283 --> 00:01:17,822
What runs on a machine you own is real;

22
00:01:17,822 --> 00:01:21,011
every cloud step stops
at its first honest error,

23
00:01:21,011 --> 00:01:22,899
and we say what yours prints.

24
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:25,697
Let's draw the goal properly.

25
00:01:26,017 --> 00:01:31,297
A browser sends a request to port 80,
where nginx passes pages

26
00:01:31,297 --> 00:01:36,662
to the frontend on port 3000
and data calls to the API on 9000.

27
00:01:36,982 --> 00:01:40,502
The API asks the vector database on 8000.

28
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:43,443
The slide draws
its arrowheads the other way;

29
00:01:43,443 --> 00:01:45,657
we draw requests the way they travel.

30
00:01:45,977 --> 00:01:47,982
The slide shows four containers.

31
00:01:48,302 --> 00:01:49,367
There is a fifth:

32
00:01:49,367 --> 00:01:53,187
a loader that fills the vector database once
and then leaves.

33
00:01:53,433 --> 00:01:58,151
The lecture splits the manual route
into four stages: push the images,

34
00:01:58,151 --> 00:02:02,734
set up the VM, deploy the containers,
and put a web server in front.

35
00:02:03,054 --> 00:02:04,868
That is our map for part 1.

36
00:02:05,116 --> 00:02:09,724
The README starts with a check,
not a command: your containers run locally.

37
00:02:10,044 --> 00:02:15,197
The vector database is up,
the API shows its docs on port 9000,

38
00:02:15,197 --> 00:02:18,386
and the frontend its home page on 3000.

39
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:24,329
A small note: on this code, /status
is a better API check than the docs page.

40
00:02:24,649 --> 00:02:28,532
It answers version 3.0,
so you know which code runs.

41
00:02:28,766 --> 00:02:34,035
If you followed lecture 18 lately,
it now teaches a refactored version 2;

42
00:02:34,035 --> 00:02:38,004
here you run version 3,
with this repository's scripts.

43
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:41,893
We follow main,
the branch the course site links;

44
00:02:41,893 --> 00:02:45,610
on recording day it was this
November 2025 commit.

45
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:49,066
READMEs change;
git log shows which one you have.

46
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:52,248
Stage 1: push the images.

47
00:02:52,568 --> 00:02:56,249
Create an access token
in your Docker Hub account settings;

48
00:02:56,249 --> 00:02:59,181
it is a password for scripts,
so never show it.

49
00:02:59,417 --> 00:03:03,336
Typed exactly as the README prints it,
the login fails:

50
00:03:03,336 --> 00:03:06,542
syntax error near
unexpected token `newline'.

51
00:03:06,862 --> 00:03:09,712
Angle brackets
are redirections to the shell,

52
00:03:09,712 --> 00:03:13,006
so replace the whole placeholder,
brackets included.

53
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:17,666
Better: the flag --password-stdin keeps
the token out of your shell history.

54
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:21,207
Now the Docker Hub script,
one in each service folder.

55
00:03:21,527 --> 00:03:27,159
Line 11 sets the user name to dlops,
the course account: change it to yours.

56
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:30,925
Line 27 creates
and selects a new BuildKit builder;

57
00:03:30,925 --> 00:03:34,035
the script builds,
then builds again to push.

58
00:03:34,355 --> 00:03:42,803
The API script builds for AMD64 and ARM64;
the frontend script, AMD64 only.

59
00:03:43,050 --> 00:03:46,805
Inside frontend-react,
outside any docker shell, run it.

60
00:03:47,125 --> 00:03:51,690
It prints dlops, creates the builder,
and builds: Compiled successfully.

61
00:03:52,010 --> 00:03:54,719
The warning says this build
stays in the builder cache.

62
00:03:54,967 --> 00:03:56,972
Then the push: access denied.

63
00:03:57,292 --> 00:03:59,588
We are not dlops, and not logged in;

64
00:03:59,588 --> 00:04:03,095
with your name and login,
this is where it says pushed.

65
00:04:03,333 --> 00:04:06,703
Run the same script
in api-service and vector-db;

66
00:04:06,703 --> 00:04:09,797
we did not capture
those two-platform builds.

67
00:04:10,117 --> 00:04:14,823
Those two build for two chip types,
one of them emulated:

68
00:04:14,823 --> 00:04:20,272
expect a long api-service build,
and on Linux register QEMU first.

69
00:04:20,592 --> 00:04:23,301
Pause, run all three, and check Docker Hub.

70
00:04:23,550 --> 00:04:26,237
Skip this stage and part 1 still works,

71
00:04:26,237 --> 00:04:31,059
using the public dlops images,
which are a little older than the code.

72
00:04:31,379 --> 00:04:36,201
If you pushed yours, replace dlops
with your user name in the VM commands.

73
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:39,441
Stage 2: set up the VM.

74
00:04:39,761 --> 00:04:44,755
In the console,
create a VM instance with the defaults,

75
00:04:44,755 --> 00:04:50,747
except machine type N2D, a 50 GB disk,
and HTTP and HTTPS allowed.

76
00:04:50,983 --> 00:04:56,851
Those two ticks add
network tags, http-server and https-server;

77
00:04:56,851 --> 00:05:02,439
the default firewall
opens 80 and 443 only to VMs with them.

78
00:05:02,759 --> 00:05:06,557
The VM now bills while it runs,
its disk while it exists.

79
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,344
Pause, create yours,

80
00:05:08,344 --> 00:05:12,667
and SSH in with the SSH button
in the VM instances list.

81
00:05:12,987 --> 00:05:16,599
Our VM is a stand-in
we can capture end to end:

82
00:05:16,599 --> 00:05:19,749
a fresh Debian 12 machine without Docker.

83
00:05:20,069 --> 00:05:22,352
Every command is the same on yours.

84
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:27,677
Install Docker with the official script:
download it with curl, run it with sudo.

85
00:05:27,997 --> 00:05:33,821
It adds the Docker repository for bookworm
and installs the engine, buildx and compose.

86
00:05:34,633 --> 00:05:37,812
Then check it with sudo docker --version.

87
00:05:38,132 --> 00:05:40,841
Pause here and install Docker on your VM.

88
00:05:41,083 --> 00:05:42,729
Next, create folders:

89
00:05:42,729 --> 00:05:47,590
persistent-folder, secrets and conf/nginx,
writable by anyone.

90
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:52,966
The README lists these six commands twice:
run them once, from your home directory.

91
00:05:55,667 --> 00:05:58,699
An extra check: the folders belong to root,

92
00:05:58,699 --> 00:06:02,366
but anyone may write there:
fine for a demo VM only.

93
00:06:02,686 --> 00:06:06,461
Stay in your home directory:
later mounts use the current folder.

94
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:11,151
Now the app's key,
gcp-service.json, written with echo;

95
00:06:11,151 --> 00:06:13,740
ours holds just the placeholder.

96
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:18,384
Paste in the gcp-service key
from your laptop's secrets folder;

97
00:06:18,384 --> 00:06:20,375
part 2 shows how to make one.

98
00:06:20,695 --> 00:06:22,252
Never show or commit it.

99
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:25,703
Last, create the Docker network,
cheese-app-network:

100
00:06:25,703 --> 00:06:29,583
a private network where our containers
find each other by name.

101
00:06:29,903 --> 00:06:31,823
Pause and run these three steps.

102
00:06:32,967 --> 00:06:34,844
Stage 3: the containers.

103
00:06:35,164 --> 00:06:39,538
Pull the Chroma image, then stop
and remove any old vector database.

104
00:06:39,858 --> 00:06:42,934
The error,
No such container, is harmless here:

105
00:06:42,934 --> 00:06:45,618
|| true makes the step safe to run again.

106
00:06:45,867 --> 00:06:50,626
Then run it detached
on cheese-app-network, publishing port 8000,

107
00:06:50,626 --> 00:06:53,995
restarting always,
with a data folder mounted.

108
00:06:54,315 --> 00:06:56,384
Its name goes into the phone book.

109
00:06:56,750 --> 00:07:00,431
An extra check: the port is published,
the heartbeat answers,

110
00:07:00,431 --> 00:07:03,086
Chroma version one at the time of recording.

111
00:07:03,334 --> 00:07:06,150
Now the loader, run vector db cli.

112
00:07:06,470 --> 00:07:11,099
It finds the database by name,
cheese-app-vector-db, not by address.

113
00:07:11,419 --> 00:07:15,003
The project is the README's default;
change it to yours.

114
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:21,074
It downloads, creates the collection,
and loads eleven files:

115
00:07:21,074 --> 00:07:23,079
14,356 chunks in all.

116
00:07:23,399 --> 00:07:27,474
The embeddings come precomputed,
so our placeholder key never mattered.

117
00:07:27,717 --> 00:07:31,472
The loader ran with the --rm flag,
so it exits and is gone.

118
00:07:31,792 --> 00:07:34,010
Pause and run both containers now.

119
00:07:34,250 --> 00:07:35,253
A question.

120
00:07:35,573 --> 00:07:36,895
The loader is gone.

121
00:07:37,215 --> 00:07:40,970
Are the 14,356 chunks gone too?

122
00:07:50,117 --> 00:07:52,674
No. The loader only sent them;

123
00:07:52,674 --> 00:07:56,389
they sit in the vector database container,
which keeps running.

124
00:07:56,709 --> 00:07:59,226
Remove it,
and the loader must fill a new one.

125
00:07:59,467 --> 00:08:01,387
But where inside that container?

126
00:08:01,707 --> 00:08:07,552
A small slip: Chroma saves to /data,
so the folder the README mounts stays empty.

127
00:08:07,872 --> 00:08:11,665
To keep the data on the VM disk,
mount /data instead,

128
00:08:11,665 --> 00:08:15,531
rerun the stop, remove and run lines,
then the loader.

129
00:08:16,667 --> 00:08:18,608
Next, run api-service.

130
00:08:18,928 --> 00:08:23,046
The image is not on the VM yet,
so docker run pulls it first.

131
00:08:23,284 --> 00:08:26,361
Two mounts, persistent-folder and secrets;

132
00:08:26,361 --> 00:08:30,025
port 9000; the key path;
and the database by name.

133
00:08:30,345 --> 00:08:32,563
The project is the README's default,

134
00:08:32,563 --> 00:08:35,337
and the bucket name is passed
but never used.

135
00:08:38,450 --> 00:08:43,591
The logs show loading CNN model,
then uvicorn running on port 9000.

136
00:08:43,911 --> 00:08:47,112
The model comes from a GitHub release,
not a bucket,

137
00:08:47,112 --> 00:08:49,821
so our picture grays out the slide's bucket.

138
00:08:50,067 --> 00:08:53,551
And the image is big,
about five gigabytes on disk,

139
00:08:53,551 --> 00:08:55,123
with TensorFlow inside.

140
00:08:55,367 --> 00:08:57,415
Three extra curl checks:

141
00:08:57,415 --> 00:09:02,706
the root says Welcome to AC215,
and /docs shows the docs page.

142
00:09:03,026 --> 00:09:07,612
/status says 404:
the public image is older than your clone.

143
00:09:07,850 --> 00:09:10,794
The README then offers
an interactive variant.

144
00:09:11,114 --> 00:09:15,253
Run as written, it fails:
the name api-service is in use,

145
00:09:15,253 --> 00:09:18,304
because the detached container still runs.

146
00:09:18,550 --> 00:09:23,457
Remove the running one with docker rm -f,
and the variant opens a shell.

147
00:09:23,777 --> 00:09:27,553
It is optional:
we exit and start the detached one again.

148
00:09:28,850 --> 00:09:31,453
Then the frontend, on port 3000.

149
00:09:31,773 --> 00:09:37,086
The extra check shows the title, Formaggio,
and the base API URL,

150
00:09:37,086 --> 00:09:41,501
/api-service,
which the page calls on its own address.

151
00:09:41,821 --> 00:09:43,250
Pause and start both.

152
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:46,747
Stage 4: a web server in front.

153
00:09:47,067 --> 00:09:50,691
A reverse proxy gives
the browser one address:

154
00:09:50,691 --> 00:09:55,813
nginx listens on port 80 and forwards
each request inside the VM.

155
00:09:56,050 --> 00:09:58,803
In the repository's nginx.conf,

156
00:09:58,803 --> 00:10:05,287
the location block on line 35 catches paths
that start with /api-service.

157
00:10:05,607 --> 00:10:09,255
It passes them to api-service on port 9000.

158
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:12,103
Look at the end of that address: one slash.

159
00:10:12,423 --> 00:10:17,159
With it, nginx swaps the matched prefix
for that slash,

160
00:10:17,159 --> 00:10:21,895
so /api-service/status reaches the API
as just /status.

161
00:10:22,133 --> 00:10:26,293
Everything else goes to the next block,
the frontend on 3000.

162
00:10:26,613 --> 00:10:30,219
Both targets are container names
on cheese-app-network.

163
00:10:30,467 --> 00:10:34,414
The README writes this file
with one long echo in single quotes.

164
00:10:34,734 --> 00:10:37,019
But the file holds single quotes too,

165
00:10:37,019 --> 00:10:41,219
so the shell eats the log_format
and leaves a dash and two brackets.

166
00:10:41,517 --> 00:10:44,760
Routing still works,
but the access logs are useless.

167
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:49,965
Write it with a quoted heredoc instead:
cat, the same text, then EOF.

168
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:53,443
If you used the echo,
rewrite the file this way first.

169
00:10:53,763 --> 00:10:57,752
Then run nginx with that file mounted,
publishing port 80.

170
00:10:58,072 --> 00:11:03,960
The container list shows four:
ports 80, 3000, 9000 and 8000.

171
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:08,693
Open the external IP of your VM,
from the VM instances list,

172
00:11:08,693 --> 00:11:11,688
in a browser, and the home page appears.

173
00:11:12,008 --> 00:11:18,216
On the VM, curl agrees:
200 OK, served by nginx, title Formaggio.

174
00:11:18,450 --> 00:11:24,381
/api-service says Welcome to AC215:
the prefix was cut on the way.

175
00:11:24,701 --> 00:11:26,322
Pause and open your site.

176
00:11:26,567 --> 00:11:27,570
A question.

177
00:11:27,890 --> 00:11:30,770
Port 9000 is published on the VM.

178
00:11:31,090 --> 00:11:32,839
Does it answer from your laptop?

179
00:11:41,683 --> 00:11:47,934
No. The two ticks opened only 80 and 443;
no rule opens 9000.

180
00:11:48,254 --> 00:11:52,702
Port 9000 is open on the VM
but shut at the network edge:

181
00:11:52,702 --> 00:11:53,950
one public door.

182
00:11:54,617 --> 00:11:55,705
Now the puzzle.

183
00:11:56,025 --> 00:12:00,377
The web server looks up frontend
and api-service once, at start.

184
00:12:00,697 --> 00:12:05,104
With the frontend stopped,
the restart fails that lookup and exits,

185
00:12:05,104 --> 00:12:07,011
taking the API route with it.

186
00:12:07,250 --> 00:12:11,943
Start the frontend, then nginx,
and the site answers 200 again.

187
00:12:12,263 --> 00:12:15,509
Start order matters,
and so does a restart policy,

188
00:12:15,509 --> 00:12:18,301
which only the vector database command has.

189
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:21,215
Let's count what we typed.

190
00:12:21,535 --> 00:12:24,959
Twenty-one commands on the VM,
plus console clicks,

191
00:12:24,959 --> 00:12:27,913
plus three Docker Hub scripts on the laptop.

192
00:12:28,233 --> 00:12:30,324
Each one added a block to the VM.

193
00:12:30,567 --> 00:12:31,932
Now three questions.

194
00:12:32,252 --> 00:12:35,964
Repeat: can you build the same thing
for a staging copy tomorrow?

195
00:12:36,284 --> 00:12:38,674
Review: what exactly is on that VM?

196
00:12:38,994 --> 00:12:41,255
Undo: how do you delete every piece?

197
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,660
The lecture answers with one idea:

198
00:12:43,660 --> 00:12:47,153
too many steps to keep track of,
so let a tool do them.

199
00:12:47,567 --> 00:12:49,402
That is infrastructure as code.

200
00:12:49,722 --> 00:12:51,272
You describe the servers,

201
00:12:51,272 --> 00:12:55,858
the networks and the software
in a real programming language, Python here,

202
00:12:55,858 --> 00:12:57,594
and keep it in Git to share.

203
00:12:57,914 --> 00:13:00,409
No YAML file, no special language.

204
00:13:00,650 --> 00:13:05,461
An earlier version of the course
did these same stages as Ansible playbooks,

205
00:13:05,461 --> 00:13:06,474
written in YAML.

206
00:13:06,794 --> 00:13:09,589
This version does them with Pulumi,
one to one.

207
00:13:09,950 --> 00:13:14,899
One more thing the README never says:
delete this VM now, before part 2.

208
00:13:15,219 --> 00:13:20,617
In the console, open VM instances,
select it, and delete it with its boot disk.

209
00:13:20,937 --> 00:13:23,838
Pause and delete yours;
the list should be empty.

210
00:13:24,083 --> 00:13:28,883
Part 2 builds it all again, as code,
and lets a tool remember every piece.

